// @check
// @error unknown type '{[boolean]:i32}' within 'i32|{[boolean]:i32}'
// @error unknown type '{[boolean]:i32}' within '({[boolean]:i32})'
// @error unknown type '{[boolean]:i32}' within '{v:{[boolean]:i32}}'
// @error unknown type '{[boolean]:i32}' within '()=>{[boolean]:i32}'
// DIAGNOSTIC WIDTH, the NEGATIVE half (D-MAPKEYDIAG): the actionable
// unsupported-key message fires only for a map spelling the annotation OPENS
// with. `error-unsupported-key.vl` pins the bare spelling and
// `error-unsupported-key-not-whole-span.vl` pins the two WIDER carriers that
// still open one (a list of maps, a nullable map); this file pins the four
// shapes that carry a boolean-keyed map WITHOUT opening with it, and they keep
// the bare "unknown type" they have always had.
//
// It is the entombment pin for the spelling-tree route. The string cut this
// replaced tested `name[0] == '{' && name[1] == '['`; the tree reads the same
// width as a descent down the LEFT SPINE (`TS_ARR` / `TS_UNION` / `TS_ISECT`
// only — see `typecheck.tsMapKeyNodeOf`). A descent that also stepped through a
// union's LATER members, a `TS_PAREN`, a `TS_OBJ` field or a `TS_FUNC` result —
// the four lines below, in that order — would turn every one of these into the
// actionable message, which the string cut never did.
let a: i32 | {[boolean]: i32} = null
let b: ({[boolean]: i32}) = null
let c: {v: {[boolean]: i32}} = null
let d: () => {[boolean]: i32} = null
